\details{
Direct coefficient comparisons are generally not a sufficient refinement
audit. A coefficient can change because the intercept or another model term
changes, while the combined fitted value for a policy remains similar.
\code{audit_refinement()} therefore compares predictions from the original and
refined models on common observed model-point combinations.

The model points are obtained with \code{\link[=rating_grid]{rating_grid()}}. Portfolio and level
results are weighted by the number of records or, when supplied, by
\code{exposure}. With \code{scale = "per_exposure"}, predictions that include an
exposure offset are divided by total exposure after aggregation. This gives
an exposure-weighted fitted rate rather than an unweighted average over
unique model points.

The resulting measure should be named according to the model being audited.
For a frequency model it is normally a fitted frequency; for a severity
model it is a fitted average severity; and for a direct risk-premium model it
can be labelled \code{"risk_premium"}. A complete risk-premium comparison requires
either a direct risk-premium model or an explicit combination of frequency
and severity predictions.

Individual changes are \code{after / before - 1} on identical rows and the
response scale. Non-positive baselines, negative refitted responses and
non-finite pairs are excluded and counted. Quantiles use the inverse
weighted empirical distribution. The weighted average of these percentage
changes is not the percentage change in portfolio predictions. To isolate
tariff structure, apply \code{\link[=adjust_intercept]{adjust_intercept()}} to align the candidate
portfolio level before auditing; the audit never calibrates automatically.
}
